1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,296
This is ECE 358, video 8 b. The topic is pipelining and Go-Back-N. It covers Chapter 3, slides 30 to 37. It starts with a short review of stop-and-wait performance.

2
00:00:15,746 --> 00:00:33,623
After this video, you can explain why stop-and-wait has a low utilization. You can calculate the sender utilization with W packets in flight, as in problem set 3. And you can explain the Go-Back-N sender window, cumulative ACKs, and timeouts.

3
00:00:34,073 --> 00:00:42,180
Here is the time diagram of slide 30. It is not to scale. The sender transmits the first bit at time 0.

4
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:45,894
It transmits the last bit at time L over R.

5
00:00:46,344 --> 00:00:51,677
The bits travel for the propagation delay. The first bit arrives after d prop.

6
00:00:52,127 --> 00:00:57,973
The last bit arrives at L over R plus d prop. Then the receiver sends the ACK.

7
00:00:58,423 --> 00:01:01,857
The slides take the ACK transmission time as zero.

8
00:01:02,307 --> 00:01:09,091
The ACK travels back for another d prop. It arrives at the sender at L over R plus 2 d prop.

9
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:17,776
RTT is the round-trip time, 2 times d prop. So the ACK arrives at RTT plus L over R.

10
00:01:18,226 --> 00:01:21,127
Only then can the sender send the next packet.

11
00:01:21,577 --> 00:01:28,532
So in each cycle of RTT plus L over R, the sender is busy for only L over R.

12
00:01:28,982 --> 00:01:32,886
For the rest of the cycle, the RTT, the sender is idle.

13
00:01:33,336 --> 00:01:40,568
From the diagram, U sender equals L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

14
00:01:41,018 --> 00:01:48,122
The values come from slide 29. L over R is 0 point 008 ms.

15
00:01:48,572 --> 00:01:55,996
RTT is 2 times d prop. That is 2 times 15 ms, which is 30 ms.

16
00:01:56,446 --> 00:02:08,350
Put the values into the formula. U sender is 0 point 008 ms, divided by 30 ms plus 0 point 008 ms.

17
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:18,976
That is 0 point 008 divided by 30 point 008, which is about 0 point 0 0 0 2 7.

18
00:02:19,426 --> 00:02:23,671
As a percentage, it is 0 point 0 2 7 percent.

19
00:02:24,121 --> 00:02:32,655
The bar shows one cycle of 30 point 008 ms, to scale. The orange part is the wait for the ACK.

20
00:02:33,105 --> 00:02:37,883
The blue busy part, L over R, is too thin to see. Here it is zoomed.

21
00:02:38,333 --> 00:02:53,608
30 point 008 divided by 0 point 008 is 3751. So the busy part is only 1 part in 3751 of the cycle.

22
00:02:54,058 --> 00:02:57,983
The throughput is the utilization times the link rate R.

23
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:11,959
0 point 0 0 0 2 7 times 10 to the power 9 bits per second is 270 thousand bits per second. That is 270 kilobits per second.

24
00:03:12,409 --> 00:03:24,654
With the exact value of U, it is 266 point 6 kilobits per second, over a 1 gigabit per second link. The protocol limits the performance of the link.

25
00:03:25,104 --> 00:03:32,080
The problem is the idle time. In stop-and-wait, only one packet is in flight. The sender waits.

26
00:03:32,530 --> 00:03:39,485
The solution is pipelining. The sender allows multiple in-flight packets that are not yet acknowledged.

27
00:03:39,935 --> 00:03:44,436
The motivation is to increase the sender utilization and the throughput.

28
00:03:44,886 --> 00:03:53,931
Pipelining needs two changes. First, the range of sequence numbers must increase. Each in-flight packet needs its own number.

29
00:03:54,381 --> 00:04:03,576
Second, the sender and the receiver need buffers. The sender keeps the unACKed packets. The receiver can keep out-of-order packets.

30
00:04:04,026 --> 00:04:09,423
Here is slide 33. The sender transmits 3 packets, one after the other.

31
00:04:09,873 --> 00:04:13,479
The last bit of packet 3 leaves at 3 L over R.

32
00:04:13,929 --> 00:04:17,406
The packets arrive at the receiver one after the other.

33
00:04:17,856 --> 00:04:20,629
The receiver sends an ACK for each packet.

34
00:04:21,079 --> 00:04:28,247
The first ACK arrives at the sender at RTT plus L over R. Then the sender can send the next packet.

35
00:04:28,697 --> 00:04:32,964
So in one cycle, the sender is busy for 3 L over R.

36
00:04:33,414 --> 00:04:35,867
It is idle for the rest of the cycle.

37
00:04:36,317 --> 00:04:41,544
3-packet pipelining increases the utilization by a factor of 3.

38
00:04:41,994 --> 00:04:53,578
Here are the numbers of slide 33. With 3 packets in each cycle, U sender is 3 times L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

39
00:04:54,028 --> 00:05:01,281
3 times 0 point 008 ms is 0 point 024 ms.

40
00:05:01,731 --> 00:05:08,857
Divide by 30 point 008. U sender is 0 point 0 0 0 8 0.

41
00:05:09,307 --> 00:05:17,392
The slide writes 0 point 0 0 2 4 on top. That is a typo for 0 point 0 2 4.

42
00:05:17,842 --> 00:05:28,551
The slide also writes 0 point 0 0 0 8 1. That is 3 times the rounded value 0 point 0 0 0 2 7.

43
00:05:29,001 --> 00:05:38,708
In general, with W packets in flight, U sender is W times L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R.

44
00:05:39,158 --> 00:05:40,971
U cannot be more than 1.

45
00:05:41,421 --> 00:05:51,725
The bar shows one cycle, not to scale. Each box is one L over R. With W equal to 3, the sender sends 3 packets and then waits.

46
00:05:52,175 --> 00:05:59,450
If W is large enough, the packets fill the whole cycle. Then the sender is never idle, and U is 1.

47
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:17,948
This needs W equal to RTT plus L over R, divided by L over R. In the slide example, that is 30 point 008 divided by 0 point 008, which is 3751 packets.

48
00:06:18,398 --> 00:06:26,398
There are two pipelined protocols, also called sliding window protocols. They differ in how they handle errors.

49
00:06:26,848 --> 00:06:31,584
In both, the sender can have up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline.

50
00:06:32,034 --> 00:06:34,871
Here the sender sends packets 0 to 7.

51
00:06:35,321 --> 00:06:37,497
Packets 4 and 6 are lost.

52
00:06:37,947 --> 00:06:45,926
In Go-Back-N, the receiver sends an ACK for each in-order packet. It gets 0, 1, 2 and 3, and ACKs each one.

53
00:06:46,376 --> 00:06:55,933
Then 5 arrives, but 4 is missing. The receiver does not buffer out-of-sequence packets. It discards 5 and sends ack 3 again.

54
00:06:56,383 --> 00:07:00,287
The same happens for 7: discard, and ack 3 again.

55
00:07:00,737 --> 00:07:09,527
The sender treats these ACKs as cumulative ACKs. Ack 3 means that all packets up to and including packet 3 are received.

56
00:07:09,977 --> 00:07:19,513
In Selective Repeat, the receiver sends an individual ACK for each packet that it receives: 0, 1, 2, 3, 5 and 7.

57
00:07:19,963 --> 00:07:23,184
It buffers the out-of-sequence packets 5 and 7.

58
00:07:23,634 --> 00:07:36,690
The timers are also different. The Go-Back-N sender has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. If it expires, the sender retransmits all unACKed packets.

59
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:50,260
The Selective Repeat sender has one timer for each unACKed packet. If a timer expires, it retransmits only that packet. The next video covers Selective Repeat in detail.

60
00:07:50,710 --> 00:07:55,659
Now the Go-Back-N sender. Each packet header has a k-bit sequence number.

61
00:07:56,109 --> 00:08:03,981
For k equal to 5, there are 2 to the power 5 numbers. That is 32 numbers, from 0 to 31.

62
00:08:04,431 --> 00:08:12,197
The sender sorts the sequence numbers into four groups. Green means already ACKed: here 0 to 7.

63
00:08:12,647 --> 00:08:17,639
Orange means sent, but the ACK is not received yet: here 8 to 14.

64
00:08:18,089 --> 00:08:22,185
Blue means usable, not sent yet: here 15 to 19.

65
00:08:22,635 --> 00:08:25,835
White means not usable: here 20 and higher.

66
00:08:26,285 --> 00:08:34,178
The window is a range of up to N consecutive sequence numbers for unACKed packets. Here N is 12.

67
00:08:34,628 --> 00:08:40,516
The window starts at 8. So it ends at 8 plus 12 minus 1, which is 19.

68
00:08:40,966 --> 00:08:47,281
Two pointers mark the window. send base points to the oldest unACKed packet, 8.

69
00:08:47,731 --> 00:08:51,912
next seq num points to the next sequence number to send, 15.

70
00:08:52,362 --> 00:09:01,621
The timeout rule. On a timeout of packet n, the sender retransmits packet n and all packets with higher sequence numbers in the window.

71
00:09:02,071 --> 00:09:10,412
Here is slide 36. The first row is the window from slide 35: send base 8, next seq num 15.

72
00:09:10,862 --> 00:09:16,345
The sender receives ACKs for 3 packets: 8, 9 and 10. They turn green.

73
00:09:16,795 --> 00:09:20,848
send base moves from 8 to 8 plus 3, which is 11.

74
00:09:21,298 --> 00:09:29,639
The window slides 3 places to the right. It now covers 11 to 11 plus 12 minus 1, which is 22.

75
00:09:30,089 --> 00:09:33,609
So 20, 21 and 22 become usable.

76
00:09:34,059 --> 00:09:40,502
Then the upper layer gives the sender 2 data packets. The sender sends them as 15 and 16.

77
00:09:40,952 --> 00:09:45,773
next seq num moves from 15 to 15 plus 2, which is 17.

78
00:09:46,223 --> 00:09:51,962
Here is slide 37. send base is 11, and next seq num is 17.

79
00:09:52,412 --> 00:10:00,497
So packets 11 to 16 are sent but not ACKed. That is 17 minus 11, which is 6 packets.

80
00:10:00,947 --> 00:10:10,867
A timeout occurs. Go-Back-N goes back to send base. It retransmits packets 11, 12, 13, 14, 15 and 16.

81
00:10:11,317 --> 00:10:18,208
There is only one timer. It runs for the oldest unACKed packet, the packet at send base.

82
00:10:18,658 --> 00:10:24,077
Questions 27, 28 and 29. Pause the video and try them.

83
00:10:24,527 --> 00:10:32,825
Question 27. In a pipelining protocol, the sender can have multiple in-flight packets that are not yet acknowledged.

84
00:10:33,275 --> 00:10:38,801
Question 28. The key advantage is higher sender utilization and throughput.

85
00:10:39,251 --> 00:10:51,773
With 3 packets per round trip, the utilization is 3 times larger: 0 point 0 0 0 8 0 instead of 0 point 0 0 0 2 7.

86
00:10:52,223 --> 00:10:55,957
Question 29. Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat.

87
00:10:56,407 --> 00:11:00,439
Question 30 asks for a table. Pause the video and try it.

88
00:11:00,889 --> 00:11:07,545
Row 1. In both protocols, the sender can have up to N unACKed packets in the pipeline.

89
00:11:07,995 --> 00:11:16,827
Row 2. The Go-Back-N receiver ACKs each in-order packet. The Selective Repeat receiver ACKs each correctly received packet.

90
00:11:17,277 --> 00:11:23,335
Row 3. Go-Back-N discards out-of-order packets. Selective Repeat buffers them.

91
00:11:23,785 --> 00:11:30,143
Row 4. A Go-Back-N ACK is cumulative. A Selective Repeat ACK is individual.

92
00:11:30,593 --> 00:11:40,705
Row 5. Go-Back-N has one timer, for the oldest unACKed packet. Selective Repeat has one timer per unACKed packet.

93
00:11:41,155 --> 00:11:51,501
Row 6. On a timeout, Go-Back-N resends all unACKed packets. Selective Repeat resends only the packet whose timer expired.

94
00:11:51,951 --> 00:12:06,949
Problem 1 b continues problem 1. At 1 gigabit per second, d is 6 point 7 2 microseconds, and T is 16 point 0 0 7 ms. Pause the video and try it.

95
00:12:07,399 --> 00:12:18,791
With a window of W frames, the sender sends W frames in each cycle T. So the efficiency is the minimum of W d over T, and 1.

96
00:12:19,241 --> 00:12:22,483
The throughput is W over T frames per second.

97
00:12:22,933 --> 00:12:40,192
W equal to 10. W d over T is 10 times 6 point 7 2 microseconds, divided by 16 thousand and 7 microseconds. That is 0 point 0 0 4 2, or 0 point 4 2 percent.

98
00:12:40,642 --> 00:12:50,370
The throughput is 10 divided by 0 point 0 1 6 0 0 7 seconds, which is 625 frames per second.

99
00:12:50,820 --> 00:13:00,143
W equal to 100. W d over T is 0 point 0 4 2, so the efficiency is 4 point 2 percent.

100
00:13:00,593 --> 00:13:11,473
The throughput is 100 divided by 0 point 0 1 6 0 0 7 seconds, about 6247 frames per second.

101
00:13:11,923 --> 00:13:20,584
W equal to 1000. W d over T is 0 point 4 2, so the efficiency is 42 percent.

102
00:13:21,034 --> 00:13:26,901
The throughput is about 62 thousand 4 hundred and 73 frames per second.

103
00:13:27,351 --> 00:13:37,868
W equal to 10 thousand. W d over T is 4 point 2, more than 1. So the efficiency is 1, which is 100 percent.

104
00:13:38,318 --> 00:13:51,993
The link is full. The throughput is C over L: 10 to the power 9 divided by 6720, which is 148 thousand 8 hundred and 10 frames per second.

105
00:13:52,443 --> 00:13:57,243
The ideal window just fills the cycle: W equals T over d.

106
00:13:57,693 --> 00:14:07,527
16 thousand and 7 microseconds divided by 6 point 7 2 microseconds is 2382 frames.

107
00:14:07,977 --> 00:14:25,577
The solution writes 2381, because it rounds T to 16 ms. 16 thousand microseconds divided by 6 point 7 2 microseconds is 2381. Both give the same idea.

108
00:14:26,027 --> 00:14:43,137
Here is the recap. One. U sender is L over R, divided by RTT plus L over R. In the slide example, it is 0 point 0 2 7 percent, so the throughput is only 270 kilobits per second.

109
00:14:43,587 --> 00:14:57,517
Two. Pipelining puts many unACKed packets in flight. It needs more sequence numbers and buffers. With W packets in flight, the utilization is W times larger, up to 1.

110
00:14:57,967 --> 00:15:08,421
Three. In the problem set form, the efficiency with a window is the minimum of W d over T, and 1. The ideal window is T over d.

111
00:15:08,871 --> 00:15:18,663
Four. Go-Back-N uses cumulative ACKs, no receiver buffer, and one timer. A timeout resends the packet and all later sent packets.

112
00:15:19,113 --> 00:15:32,275
Five. The window has send base, next seq num, and size N. ACKs slide it to the right. The next video covers Selective Repeat and the window size. That is the end of video 8 b.
